Commit: 8fb7166579140e25ef288359315245caa0ea47aa

> * **Backwards compatibility** - all existing `href` usage unchanged

@bgub To be clear, I'm assuming this means `href` props become **\*untyped\*** with `experimental.typedRoutes`, right?

Reading the PR description and changes, I'm assuming that the current [`experimental.typedRoutes`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/typedRoutes) functionality of providing TS type errors on simple `href` strings like the ones below will be removed, correct?

```tsx
// Current experimental.typedRoutes functionality

// ✅
<Link href="/about" />

// ❌ Type '"/aboot"' is not assignable to type ...
<Link href="/aboot" />

// ✅
<Link href="/projects/${slug}" />

// ❌ Type '`/projectsss/${string}`' is not assignable to type ...
<Link href="/projectsss/${slug}" />

// ✅
<Link href="/projects/${projectSlug}/tasks/${taskSlug}" />

// ❌ Type '`/projectsss/${string}`/tasks/${string}' is not assignable to type ...
<Link href="/projectsss/${projectSlug}/tasks/${taskSlug}" />
```

As far as I can tell, the new version would be like this:

```tsx
// New experimental.typedRoutes functionality

// ✅
<Link path="/about" />

// ❌ Type '"/aboot"' is not assignable to type ...
<Link path="/aboot" />

// ✅
<Link path="/projects/[slug]" params={{ slug: slug }} />

// ❌ Type '`/projectsss/${string}`' is not assignable to type ...
<Link path="/projectsss/[slug]" params={{ slug: slug }} />

// ✅
<Link path="/projects/[projectSlug]/tasks/[taskSlug]" params={{
  projectSlug: projectSlug,
  taskSlug: taskSlug,
}} />

// ❌ Type '`/projectsss/${string}`/tasks/${string}' is not assignable to type ...
<Link path="/projectsss/[projectSlug]/tasks/[taskSlug]" params={{
  projectSlug: projectSlug,
  taskSlug: taskSlug,
}} />
```

The new API makes things much more verbose and complex 🤔 Even with using object shorthand notation:

```tsx
<Link path="/projects/[projectSlug]/tasks/[taskSlug]" params={{ projectSlug, taskSlug }} />
```

Single `href` strings are really great for learning and ergonomics, too bad if we would have to lose them.

